ke/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070232/state.json" "file_sha256" "be6dc21a294a0d1b73c224ae73ac3fae765ba877b364b5d6a82506f3469b6309"} {"question" "q-343245" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070232/state.json" "file_sha256" "be6dc21a294a0d1b73c224ae73ac3fae765ba877b364b5d6a82506f3469b6309"} {"question" "q-343251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=12x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-2∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n12x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070232/state.json" "file_sha256" "be6dc21a294a0d1b73c224ae73ac3fae765ba877b364b5d6a82506f3469b6309"} {"question" "q-343251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-2) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ 12x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-72(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070232/state.json" "file_sha256" "be6dc21a294a0d1b73c224ae73ac3fae765ba877b364b5d6a82506f3469b6309"} {"question" "q-343251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070232/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6e871d80cfa235b9b0b5332a2a10330f89050a626ed75eae4f97e1ff6c6d377f"} {"question" "q-343251" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070232/state.json" "file_sha256" "be6dc21a294a0d1b73c224ae73ac3fae765ba877b364b5d6a82506f3469b6309"} {"qu